<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banteay Kdei</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prasat Banteay Kdei, Citadel of Chambers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banteay Kdei, the Citadel of Chambers, is a Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> and east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, built between the mid-12th and early 13th centuries during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>. A monastic complex in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> style, similar in plan to Ta Prohm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preah-khan">Preah Khan</a> but smaller and less complex, it stands on the site of a 10th-century temple of Rajendravarman, and inscriptions record its building by Jayavarman VII and the royal architect Kavindrarimathana. Monks occupied it at intervals over the centuries until the 1960s. Faulty construction and poor sandstone have left it dilapidated, and it is now under renovation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banteay-kdei.jpg" alt="Banteay Kdei" width="330" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banteay_Kdei,_Angkor,_Camboya,_2013-08-16,_DD_10.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the reign of Jayavarman VII alone Ta Prohm, Preah Khan, <b>Banteay Kdei</b> and Neak Pean were built, beside the Bayon at the centre of Angkor Thom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
